{{Short description|Type of atonal acoustic musical instrument}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=August 2023}}
[[File:Thomas Bloch - Fénétrange - 19-09-2009 - Waterphone.jpg | thumb | right | alt= Musician [[Thomas Bloch]] playing the waterphone, 19 September 2009 at the Mittersheim pond in France |
Musician [[Thomas Bloch]] playing the waterphone, 19 September 2009 at the Mittersheim pond, France]]
A '''waterphone''' (also '''ocean harp''') is a type of [[inharmonic]] acoustic tuned [[idiophone]] consisting of a [[stainless steel]] resonator bowl or pan with a cylindrical neck and bronze rods of different lengths and diameters around the rim of the bowl. The [[resonator]] may contain a small amount of water giving the waterphone a vibrant ethereal sound that has appeared in movie soundtracks, record albums, and live performances. The instrument was invented, developed, and manufactured by American [[Richard Waters]] (1935–2013).<ref>{{cite web |last1=Waters |first1=Richard |title=The Waterphone Story |archive-date=9 October 2016 |url=http://www.waterphone.com/story.php |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161009124815/http://www.waterphone.com/story.php |access-date=9 October 2016}}</ref>

The waterphone was available in four sizes: the Standard (7" diameter), the Whaler (12" diameter), the Bass (14" diameter), and the MegaBass (16" diameter).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.waterphone.com/prices.php |title=Waterphone Online – The Official Website of The Waterphone |website=www.waterphone.com |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319084700/http://www.waterphone.com/prices.php |archive-date=19 March 2012}}</ref> It is generally played in a seated position by a soloist and either bowed or drummed, played as a [[friction idiophone|friction]] or [[struck idiophone]], with movements to affect the water inside. This combines the resonant characteristics of the bowl and rods in combination with the movement of the water. The sound of the waterphone is often used to evoke mystery and suspense. A [[superball]] mallet has become the prime way of drumming the waterphone.

The waterphone is a modern invention influenced by a Tibetan drum—encountered by the inventor in the early sixties—containing a small amount of water affecting its timbre.<ref>''[[Journal SEAMUS]]: The Journal of the Society for Electro-Acoustic Music in the United States'', Volume 17, 2005, p.41.</ref> It is also related to the [[nail violin]], which also used a resonator and rods (nails), and is struck or bowed.<ref>{{Cite book|last1=Heron-Allen|first1=E.|url=http://www.oxfordmusiconline.com/grovemusic/view/10.1093/gmo/9781561592630.001.0001/omo-9781561592630-e-0000019539|title=Nail violin|last2=Davies|first2=Hugh|date=2001|publisher=Oxford University Press|volume=1|language=en|doi=10.1093/gmo/9781561592630.article.19539|isbn=978-1-56159-263-0 }}</ref>

==Use==
[[File:Alex wong playing waterphone.jpg|thumb|Percussionist Alex Wong bowing a Standard waterphone]]

The waterphone has been exhibited in museums and galleries and is the subject of several short documentaries including "Art Notes," aired on public television in San Francisco, and "Celestial Wave," a movie short.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.waterphone.com/howandwho.php|title=Waterphone Online – The Official Website of The Waterphone|last=Nyrix|first=Jason Maurer|website=www.waterphone.com|access-date=16 May 2017|archive-date=12 May 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170512105909/http://www.waterphone.com/howandwho.php|url-status=dead}}</ref>  Over recent decades the waterphone has become popular with symphonies, touring bands, and recording studios. Contemporary classical composers who have written parts for waterphone in compositions include [[Sofia Gubaidulina]],<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://waterphone.digiprolocal.com/|title = Waterphone|last = nileforest|website = waterphone.digiprolocal.com|access-date = 14 March 2016|archive-date = 15 March 2016|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160315000421/http://waterphone.digiprolocal.com/|url-status = dead}}</ref> [[Jerry Goldsmith]],<ref name="Cool">{{cite web|url=http://coolmusicinstrument.com/richard-waters-and-eerie-sound-waterphone |title=Richard Waters and the eerie sound of the WATERPHONE |website=Cool Music Instrument |date=17 September 2013 |access-date=27 October 2013}}</ref> [[John Mackey (composer)|John Mackey]], [[Dan Forrest]], [[Christopher Rouse (composer)|Christopher Rouse]], [[Colin Matthews]], [[John Woolrich]], [[Carson Cooman]], [[Andi Spicer]], [[Ludovico Einaudi]], [[Andrew Carter (composer)|Andrew Carter]], [[Jörg Widmann]],<ref name="Schott Music h181">{{cite web | title=Danse macabre | website=[[Schott Music]] | url=https://www.schott-music.com/de/danse-macabre-no453734.html | language=de | access-date=4 August 2023}}</ref> [[Bernie Krause]] of [[Beaver & Krause]], and [[Todd Barton]].

The instrument has also been used prominently by rock musicians. [[Siouxsie Sioux]]'s drummer and percussionist, [[Budgie (musician)|Budgie]], used it on [[the Creatures]]' 1983 album, ''[[Feast (The Creatures album)|Feast]]''.<ref>Birch, Ian (28 April 1983). "Bamboo Music [an unterview with the Creatures]". ''Smash Hits''. </ref>

Canadian musician and composer, [[Robert Minden]], has been composing for his collection of five vintage waterphones on many recordings since the mid-1980s. His ensemble, [[The Robert Minden Ensemble]],<ref>"[http://lostsound.com/rmd/default.html Robert Minden Duo] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180928220710/http://lostsound.com/rmd/default.html |date=28 September 2018 }}", LostSound.com. Accessed 16 August 2014.</ref> formed with daughters Andrea and Dewi Minden and colleagues Carla Hallett and Nancy Walker in 1986, features the waterphone as a central instrument within their 'found object' orchestra.

The waterphone is used to great effect in [[Howard Goodall]]'s ''The Dreaming'', a musical commissioned by The [[National Youth Music Theatre]] of Great Britain, based on Shakespeare's ''A Midsummer Night's Dream''.{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} Goodall uses its ethereal sounds to evoke the mystery of the woods. In [[Derek Bourgeois]]' Symphony No 59 – Percussion symphony, which requires 16 percussion players there is a very prominent part for waterphone.

The waterphone has been featured in the soundtracks to many movies, including ''[[Poltergeist (1982 film)|Poltergeist]]'',<ref name="Cool"/> ''[[Let the Right One In (film)|Let the Right One In]]'' (2008),<ref name="Movies">{{cite web |author=6/24/11 2:40pm 6/24/11 2:40pm |url=http://io9.com/waterphone/ |title=Waterphone News, Videos, Reviews and Gossip |website=io9 |access-date=27 October 2013 |archive-date=11 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130211083739/http://io9.com/waterphone/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> ''[[The Matrix]]'',<ref name="Movies"/> ''[[Star Trek: The Motion Picture]],''<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=OIoUAQAAIAAJ|title=The Music of Star Trek|last=Bond|first=Jeff|date=1999|publisher=Lone Eagle Publishing Company|isbn=9781580650120|pages=106|language=en}}</ref> ''[[Dark Water (2002 film)|Dark Water]]'',{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} ''[[Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon (soundtrack)|Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon]]'',<ref name="Movies"/> ''[[Aliens (film)|ALIENS]]'',{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} ''The Spirit'',{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} ''Female Perversion'',{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} as well as TV production ''24''.{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} [[Tan Dun]]'s opera ''[[The First Emperor]]'' (2006) & "Water Music" feature the waterphone.{{citation needed|date=August 2014}}<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.oxfordmusiconline.com/view/10.1093/gmo/9781561592630.001.0001/omo-9781561592630-e-4002215099|title=Waterphone {{!}} Grove Music|website=www.oxfordmusiconline.com|year=2011|language=en|doi=10.1093/gmo/9781561592630.article.l2215099|access-date=23 February 2019|last1=Davies|first1=Hugh|last2=Libin|first2=Laurence|isbn=978-1-56159-263-0}}</ref> A sound sample can be found at [[Freesound|The FreeSound Project]].<ref>"[http://www.freesound.org/samplesViewSingle.php?id=14310 Waterphone 1.wav]", ''FreeSound.org''. Accessed 16 August 2014.</ref> There is a yearly "Waterphone Music Competition" sponsored by Richard Waters.{{citation needed|date=August 2014}} It has also been featured heavily as a sound effect in shows such as [[Gordon Ramsay]]'s ''[[Kitchen Nightmares]]''<ref>{{Citation |title=Ramsay_Dramatic_Violin.WAV | date=15 August 2014 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xdE2VdasvEU |language=en |access-date=9 May 2022}}</ref> and [[Hell's Kitchen (American TV series)|''Hell's Kitchen'']] (though only in the US versions), which was also used in the [[Bravo (British TV channel)|Bravo (UK)]] idents between 1997 and 1998.

The waterphone may be played in water and on several occasions has been successfully used to call whales and other cetaceans, especially by [[Jim Nollman#Interspecies|Jim Nollman]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.interspecies.com/pages/soundsystem.html |title=Interspecies Sound System |website=Interspecies.com |access-date=27 October 2013}}</ref> The true story of such interspecies communication was the basis of the stage show and album ''[[Juno Award for Children's Album of the Year|The Boy Who Wanted To Talk To Whales]]'' by The Robert Minden Ensemble in 1989.{{citation needed|date=August 2014}}

[[Jean-Michel Jarre]] has used the Hyperstellar Waterphone on his album "[[Oxymore]]" (2022).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jean-Michel Jarre: Producing Oxymore |url=https://www.soundonsound.com/people/jean-michel-jarre-producing-oxymore |access-date=2024-03-03 |website=www.soundonsound.com}}</ref>

== Development and innovation ==
The Evelyn Glennie hyperstellar Waterphone is the first double chambered waterphone. Two sound chambers are connected with a pipe and one of them has also additional pipe, which is open. Each sound chamber has a set of rods attached. The instrument is characterized by very deep bass sound and very long reverberation. It was designed by Sławek Janus and [[Evelyn Glennie]] who come up with the idea and provided some initial drawings. 

